Operational IoT Intrusion Detection under Domain Shift with a Lipschitz-Constrained MDP Decoder


Intrusion detection in operational IoT networks has two hard constraints that papers often gloss over: attacks are rare, so even a “small” false positive rate can drown a SOC in alerts, and the score you output needs to behave like a real risk estimate if it will be used for triage. This work treats those constraints as first class requirements and evaluates detectors the way they are deployed. Our protocol is simple on purpose. We train in one environment (IoT23 split A), choose the alert threshold once using only the validation negatives from that same environment, and then keep that threshold frozen. Without any re tuning, we test on IoT23 A (test), IoT23 B (a different split with a different device/family mix), and ToN IoT (a separate dataset). We start with two strong but practical baselines—logistic regression and XGBoost—and then focus on making their outputs usable: we apply Platt scaling followed by isotonic regression so that a “90%” score means something close to 90% in practice, not just “high rank.” To make the system less jumpy when the data distribution shifts, we add a small, interpretable MDP style decoder and constrain it to be 1 Lipschitz. The point is not to magically prevent performance drop under shift; it is to bound how much the decoder’s summary value can move when the input drifts within a chosen radius, so analysts can tell the difference between “real change” and “model noise.” We also release the checks needed to audit this claim (operator norms, row stochasticity tests, and measured Lipschitz constants). With thresholds fixed, the practical gap becomes obvious at the operating points that matter. IoT23 A is near perfect, but at a 1% false positive rate the true positive rate drops to 0.601 on IoT23 B and to 0.196 on ToN IoT (0.572/0.633 on IoT23 B and 0.142/0.316 on ToN IoT at 0.5%/2%). Calibration improves the trustworthiness of reported probabilities: isotonic regression reduces expected calibration error to 0.003 (IoT23 A), 0.014 (IoT23 B), and 0.077 (ToN IoT). Finally, the stability certificate is conservative but informative: across tested drift radii, observed value changes are typically one to two orders of magnitude below the certified budget, and at discount factor 0.90 (top 4 support) the transition term dominates the bound.
Keywords: IoT intrusion detection; low false positive evaluation; threshold transfer; domain shift; probability calibration; isotonic regression; Lipschitz constraint; certified stability; XGBoost; logistic regression
