1. INTRODUCTION
IoT intrusion detection must operate at very low false–positive rates (FPR) because attacks are rare and alert budgets are finite. In production security operations centers (SOCs), a seemingly modest FPR quickly translates into an unsustainable volume of benign alerts due to the base–rate problem [1]. As a result, detectors are deployed at FPRs on the order of ≤1−2%, sometimes far lower, and are expected to output calibrated probabilities so analysts can triage consistently. Classic ranking metrics (ROC–AUC, PR–AUC) are necessary but not sufficient: two models with similar AUCs can produce very different true–positive rates (TPR) at the stringent operating points that matter, and poorly calibrated scores can mislead downstream prioritization [2], [3], [4], [5]. These operational constraints motivate an evaluation and modeling approach that foregrounds low–FPR behavior and probability calibration rather than optimizing only for global ranking.
Despite progress, common evaluation practices mask overfitting and overstate generalization. A large fraction of recent IoT intrusion–detection results rely on random splits of a single public corpus, inadvertently entangling devices and attack families across train/test; protocol and capture biases further inflate apparent performance, and thresholds are often tuned post hoc on the test set. To counter these pitfalls, we construct device– and family–disjoint splits on IoT–23 (denoted IoT23–A and IoT23–B) and evaluate out of domain on ToN–IoT [6], [7]. This forces models to face unseen devices and malware families in–domain and then a shifted environment cross–domain, while our operating thresholds are fixed once on validation negatives and transferred unchanged—no test–time retuning.
We pair strong, transparent detectors with a certified analysis layer designed for stability under drift. Concretely, we train logistic regression and XGBoost baselines with class weighting on chronologically split IoT23–A, learn thresholds on IoT23–A validation negatives, and freeze and transfer those thresholds to IoT23–A test, IoT23–B, and ToN–IoT. We first apply Platt scaling and then an isotonic calibrator, which improves probability calibration without altering ranking because it is monotone [8], [9], [10], [5]. In parallel, we introduce a Lipschitz–constrained, row–local decoder that maps each 300s window to a small, interpretable MDP; simplex projections with fixed top–k support makes transitions valid and sparse, and the Lipschitz control yields a closed–form value–stability certificate for bounded input drift [11], [12], [13], [14], [15], [16], [17].
	Research Questions
	RQ1: Under fixed low–FPR thresholds, how do detectors generalize from IoT23–A to IoT23–B and ToN–IoT?
	RQ2: Does isotonic calibration maintain ranking while improving calibration, and how does calibration behave under controlled input drift?
	RQ3: Are the proposed value–stability certificates non–vacuous (good tightness/coverage), and which components dominate the bound?
	Contributions
	This paper is about making IoT intrusion detection results look like real deployment, not a clean lab split. We set the alarm threshold once using only IoT23 A validation negatives, and then carry that same threshold unchanged to IoT23 A test, IoT23 B, and ToN IoT. Instead of only reporting “overall” numbers, we report what matters in practice: TPR at very low false-positive rates (0.5/1/2% FPR), together with ROC AUC, PR AUC, and calibration error (ECE with equal mass bins) [4].
	We also treat calibration as a first class problem under domain shift. Reliability diagrams plus controlled drift probes (both random and detector aligned) show clearly when the confidence scores stay meaningful and when they start lying [18].
	On the decision side, we introduce an auditable, certifiable module: a row local 1 Lipschitz MDP decoder with simplex projections and fixed top k support. This structure lets us give deterministic, window level value stability bounds, and we make the guarantees checkable by releasing the layer wise operator norms, row stochasticity checks, and measured Lipschitz constants (global and local median). Finally, we show these certificates are not just “theory”: in experiments, the observed drops in value are typically 10–100× smaller than the certified budget, with coverage close to 1 across radii [11–15].
Results show realistic generalization gaps, strong calibration gains, and non–vacuous certificates. Under fixed thresholds (no test–time tuning), IoT23–A is near–perfect (reflecting a favorable class/operating mix), while on IoT23–B we obtain TPR@{0.5,1,2}% = {0.572,0.601,0.633}, and on ToN–IoT we obtain {0.142,0.196,0.316}. Isotonic calibration improves ECE to 0.003/0.014/0.077 on A/B/ToN–IoT without changing ROC/TPR (monotonicity). The value–stability certificates are conservative but informative: tightness ratios lie far below 1 with coverage ≈1.0 across radii, and decomposition shows the transition term dominates the bound at γ=0.90 and support k=4.
We certify stability of the decoded value (a behavior summary), not label invariance; classification may still flip near decision boundaries. Split–A’s near–separation likely reflects data composition rather than leakage: all preprocessing (z–scaler) and calibration are fit on A–train/A–val only, and devices/families are disjoint across splits by construction [6], [7]. Our detectors operate on engineered flow features (no payload or binary scanning), which aligns with many network–monitoring deployments but does not cover all intrusion modalities.
Section II situates our work within IoT IDS, calibration, and robustness. Section III details datasets, splits, detectors, calibration, robustness probes, and the certified decoder. Section IV reports low–FPR detection, calibration under drift, certificate tightness/coverage, and ablations. Section V interprets the findings, limitations, and deployment guidance, and Section VI concludes.

2. RELATED WORK
Most recent work on IoT intrusion detection evaluates on a small set of public corpora. IoT 23 provides labeled captures that mix benign device activity with diverse malware families [6], while ToN_IoT expands the setting with heterogeneous telemetry that is closer to operational environments [7]. Surveys have also noted common pitfalls—class imbalance, outdated attack mixes, and protocol bias—that make it hard to compare results across papers and to trust generalization beyond a single dataset [19]. At the same time, the base rate problem tells us that even a small false positive rate (FPR) can overwhelm analysts because attacks are rare [1]. These facts motivate an evaluation that goes beyond random splits: we separate training and testing by devices and malware families on IoT 23 and then test under domain shift on ToN_IoT [6], [7]. Our detector operates at the network–behavior level: the unit is a 300 s traffic window with engineered flow features, and the task is to decide whether that window is benign or malicious. It does not scan binaries or label individual packets in isolation; it looks for behavioral patterns that IoT malware produces on the wire.
Metrics and operating points matter when events are rare. ROC curves give a global view of the TPR–FPR trade off [2], while Precision–Recall (PR) curves are often more informative under heavy class imbalance [3]. In practice, however, SOCs run detectors at very low FPRs because of alert budgets [1]. Following this, we focus on TPR at fixed FPR (e.g., 0.5%, 1%, 2%) alongside ROC AUC/PR AUC, and we fix thresholds on a validation split and transfer them unchanged to all test sets—mirroring deployment rather than post hoc tuning.
Probability calibration is just as important as ranking. Simple, effective post hoc methods such as Platt (sigmoid) and isotonic regression map raw scores to better calibrated probabilities [8], [9], [10]; temperature scaling is a strong one parameter baseline for neural models [5]. Because histogram estimates can be biased by empty or tiny bins, we compute Expected Calibration Error (ECE) with equal mass (quantile) bins and visualize with reliability diagrams [4], [20]. Past work shows that calibration typically worsens under distribution shift [18], which is why we evaluate both before/after calibration and under controlled input drift.
Strong, transparent baselines remain useful in network security, so we include ℓ2 regularized logistic regression and XGBoost with class weighting and post hoc calibration [21], [10], [5]. All models are evaluated under the same low FPR, threshold transfer protocol described above.
A complementary line of research studies how to constrain networks so their outputs change in a controlled way when inputs are perturbed. GroupSort activations together with norm bounded linear layers yield 1 Lipschitz networks [11]; spectral normalization enforces per layer operator norms [12]; and broader regularization frameworks make Lipschitz control practical during training [13]. These ideas enable formal bounds on sensitivity, not just empirical robustness.
Our approach builds on this literature but aims at an interpretable, sequential abstraction. We introduce a Lipschitz constrained row local decoder that maps each 300 s window to a small MDP (R, P) using a 1 Lipschitz backbone and heads. We enforce row stochastic transitions by efficient projection onto the probability simplex [14] (fast related projections also exist [15]) and restrict each row to a top k support to make transitions sparse and easier to read. With verified global and local Lipschitz constants for x → (R, P), we derive formal bounds on how much the optimal value can change under bounded input drift. In plain terms: small changes in standardized features cannot cause large changes in the decoded value.
This target differs from most certification work, which aims to certify classification stability. Convex outer relaxations [22], semidefinite relaxations [23], and randomized smoothing [24] provide guarantees for logits or labels under norm bounded perturbations. We instead certify value stability of a compact MDP that analysts can inspect. The guarantee is deterministic (no sampling), benefits from row local sparsity, and plugs into a standard detection pipeline.
Finally, classical dynamic programming gives us efficient and exact value iteration on small discounted MDPs [16], [17]. Separating model learning (produce (R, P) with checked norms and valid rows) from planning (compute V⋆) lets us publish verification artifacts—layer operator norms, projection checks, and Lipschitz constants—and gives users a score whose inner workings (rewards, transitions, and value) are open to inspection.
Taken together, prior work points to three needs that we address jointly: evaluate at operational low FPRs with thresholds fixed on validation and transferred to unseen devices, unseen families, and a shifted domain [6], [7], [1]; deliver calibrated probabilities that remain useful off the i.i.d. path [8], [9], [10], [5], [4], [20], [18]; and add formal bounds through a Lipschitz constrained, row local decoder whose MDP abstraction makes behavior both effective and certifiably reliable [11], [12], [13], [14], [15], [22], [23], [24], [16], [17].
3. METHODOLOGY
This section specifies the problem, the datasets, the data processing and splitting, the baseline intrusion detectors (training, calibration, operating points), the robustness probes, and the certified value–stability analysis based on a Lipschitz–constrained row local decoder. We emphasize leak free design (train only statistics; device/family disjointness), unambiguous threshold transfer, and explicit verification artifacts that justify the formal bounds.
3.1. Problem Statement and Design Principles
Given a fixed length network traffic window x\in\operatorname{R}^d, (i) detect whether the window is malicious (y\in{0,1}) using conventional classifiers, and (ii) independently, decode an MDP that summarizes the window’s local dynamics and supports provable robustness guarantees.
Real intrusion detection is operated at very low false positive rates (FPR) because of base rate concerns [1]. Since thresholds are transferred, the achieved FPR on other domains may differ from the target rates (0.5%, 1%, 2%). Since models face device heterogeneity and dataset shift [19], [18], we (a) partition IoT 23 into device  and family disjoint splits, and (b) evaluate out of domain on ToN_IoT.
3.2.  Datasets and Splits
IoT 23 [6] is a labeled mix of benign/malicious IoT traffic with family annotations and ToN_IoT [7] is an external corpus collected in different environments and with heterogeneous attacks.
	A/B split construction (IoT 23). Let \mathcal{F}be the set of malicious families in IoT 23. We partition \mathcal{F}=\operatorname{F}_A\cup\operatorname{F}_B, \operatorname{F}_A\cap\operatorname{F}_B=\emptyset. Each device dis assigned to split A or B such that no device and no malicious family appears in both splits. Benign only devices are allocated to balance negatives. This yields two in domain but family/device disjoint evaluation sets. We persist manifests (device lists; window ranges; counts) for auditability.
	Temporal subdivision of split A. For split A only, each device timeline is cut chronologically into train/val/test partitions (≈60/20/20). We denote the held out portion as A_test. Split B and ToN_IoT are held out test sets (no tuning).
3.3. Windowing, Features, and Standardization
We operate on cached fixed windows of 300 s with 150 s stride (≈50% overlap). Each window carries engineered tabular features (flow/packet/port statistics, etc.). To avoid leakage, we fit a single z score scaler (StandardScaler) only on A train (per device chronological split) and apply it to A_val, A_test, B, and ToN_IoT. Missing engineered features are imputed with the A train mean after fitting the z scaler on A train (equivalently, we impute 0 in standardized space).
3.4. Baseline Intrusion Detectors
We train two parallel baselines on A train z features and both produce probability scores per window.
	Logistic Regression (LR): \ell_2 regularized, class_weight=balanced, solver LBFGS. It provides a linear, interpretable baseline for tabular IDS data.
	XGBoost (XGB) [21]: gradient boosted trees (e.g., n_estimators=200, max_depth=4, learning_rate=0.08, subsampling/column subsampling). It captures nonlinear interactions typical in network features.
We first apply Platt scaling (sigmoid) [8], [10] on A val to correct gross miscalibration; for final reporting we also fit isotonic regression on A val only and transfer it unchanged to all splits [9]. Because isotonic is monotone, it preserves ranking/ROC [5], [25], [4].
For each target FPR t\in{0.5,1,2}%, we compute a threshold \tau_ton the post isotonic scores as the (1-t) quantile of A val negatives:
\tau_t={\operatorname{Quantile}\funcapply}_{\mathrm{\thinsp}1-t}(s_{\mathrm{A-val}}^-).\bigm
We freeze {\tau_t}and transfer them unchanged to A_test, B, and ToN_IoT. We then report TPR@t with nonparametric bootstrap CIs (n=1000) [26], plus ROC AUC [2] and PR AUC [3]. Calibration is summarized by ECE using 15 equal mass (quantile) bins [4], [5].
3.5. Robustness Probes Under Input Drift
To quantify stability of calibration under covariate drift (distinct from the formal certificate below), we perturb standardized inputs along canonical directions and radii. Concretely, for a unit direction uand r\in[0,1](z units), we evaluate the detector on x_r=x+r\mathrm{\thinsp} uand track ECE vs. r. We consider (i) a reproducible random unit u, and (ii) detector aligned stress directions (sign flipped variants). Because isotonic is monotone, ROC/TPR at fixed FPR are unaffected; the probe isolates calibration sensitivity under drift [18].
3.6. Lipschitz–Constrained Row Local Decoder (Certified Layer)
Independently of the classifiers, we train a Lipschitz controlled decoder \Phi_\theta:\operatorname{R}^d\rightarrow(R,P)that maps each window’s z features to the parameters of a small MDP (S,A,\gamma)with \mid S\mid=8, \mid A\mid=3, \gamma=0.90[16], [17]. The outputs are:
	R\in[0,1]S×A (bounded rewards)
	P\in\operatorname{R}_+^{S\times A\times S} (row stochastic: \sum_{s^\prime}P(s,a,s^\prime)=1for all (s,a))
The backbone is a sequence of linear layers with operator norms constrained to \le1(spectral normalization) and 1 Lipschitz GroupSort activations [12], [11], [13]. Two heads (reward, transition) are likewise constrained. We enforce \parallel W\parallel_{\mathrm{op}}\le1during training and “strict clamp” at verification time.
Each transition row is projected onto the probability simplex using efficient algorithms [14], [15]. Training proceeds in two stages: (i) unmasked projection to learn coarse structure; (ii) masked projection with a fixed top k support per (s,a)(here k=4), selected by top k logits averaged on A train. An \varepsilon floor ensures exactly kpositive entries per row.
The loss encourages (a) reward alignment with the window label, (b) diagonally dominant transitions (stability), and (c) small energy:
\mathcal{L}\mathrm{\;\hairsp}=\mathrm{\;\hairsp}MSE(R, y)  +  λtrans(1-⟨P,I⟩)  +  λenergy(∥R∥22+∥P∥22).

Optimization uses AdamW, and the decoder is not a classifier; it provides a certified representation.
3.7. Value Functions and the Value–Stability Bound
For each decoded (R,P)we compute the optimal value V^\star\in\operatorname{R}^Sby exact value iteration [16]:
V^{(t+1)}(s)\mathrm{\;\hairsp}=\mathrm{\;\hairsp}{\max\funcapply}\below a(R(s,a)+\gamma\sum_{s^\prime}P(s,a,s^\prime)\mathrm{\thinsp} V^{(t)}(s^\prime)),\bigm
until \parallel V^{(t+1)}-V^{(t)}\parallel_\infty<{10}^{-6}.
Let L_Rand L_Pbe Lipschitz constants of x\mapstovec(R)and x\mapstovec(P)in \ell_2. We compute global constants by multiplying operator norms of all linear layers, and local median constants by estimating Jacobian spectral norms via power iteration on a validation batch (median across samples) [13].
With K_R=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}and K_P=\frac{2\gamma}{(1-\gamma)^2}, and row local support of size k, any z space perturbation \parallel x^\prime-x\parallel_2\le ryields the value–stability certificate
\begin{matrix}&\parallel V^\star(x^\prime)-V^\star(x)\parallel_\infty\mathrm{\;\hairsp} \le\mathrm{\;\hairsp} B(r),B(r)\mathrm{\;\hairsp} =\mathrm{\;\hairsp} (K_RL_R\mathrm{\;\hairsp} +\mathrm{\;\hairsp} K_P\mathrm{\thinsp} k\mathrm{\thinsp} L_P)\mathrm{\thinsp} r.&&\mathrm{(1)}\end{matrix}\bigm
We evaluate both global and local median variants of L_R,L_Pin (1). Only the global (L_R,L_P)produce a formal, worst case certificate in (1). The local median (L_R^{\mathrm{med}},L_P^{\mathrm{med}})are tighter heuristics; we report empirical coverage explicitly to assess conservativeness.
For tightness studies we approximate the top input singular direction of the concatenated map x\mapsto[K_R\mathrm{\thinsp}vec(R),\mathrm{\thinsp} K_Pk\mathrm{\thinsp}vec(P)]via Jacobian–vector power iteration and use it as uto form x_r=x+r\mathrm{\thinsp} u.
For radii r\in{0.01,0.02,0.04,0.06,0.08,0.1,\ldots,1.0}we compute (i) coverage =\Pr\funcapply[\Delta V^\star(x;r)\le B(r)]with both bootstrap (n=1000) and exact Clopper–Pearson binomial CIs [26], [27], and (ii) tightness =median(\Delta V^\star/B(r))with IQR.
3.8. Verification Artifacts
We release: (i) per layer operator norms for the backbone and heads, demonstrating \parallel W\parallel_{\mathrm{op}}\le1(with final strict clamp); (ii) per row transition checks confirming stochasticity (\sum_{s^\prime}P(s,a,s^\prime)=1), exact support size kenforced by an \varepsilon floor, and negligible out of support mass; and (iii) the constants {L_R,L_P}(global and local median) used in (1). These artifacts ground the certificate plots and tables.
