4. RESULTS
We report results on three held out test conditions: split A (“A_test”) and split B, both derived from IoT 23 with disjoint devices and disjoint malware families between A and B, and the external TON_IoT corpus for cross domain evaluation [6], [7]. Unless noted, we summarize (i) detection at very low false positive rates (FPR ∈ {0.5, 1, 2}%), (ii) ranking metrics (ROC AUC, PR AUC) [2], [3], (iii) probability calibration via Expected Calibration Error (ECE; 15 equal mass bins) [5], [4], (iv) robustness under controlled input drift of radius r ∈ [0, 1] in standardized (z) units, and (v) the tightness and coverage of our value stability certificates. Operating points (score thresholds) are chosen once from split A validation negatives and then transferred unchanged to all test sets (A_test, B, TON_IoT). Isotonic recalibration is monotone and thus preserves ROC/TPR while improving calibration [9], [10].
4.1. Detection at Low FPR and Baseline Calibration
Table 2 gives headline detection and calibration; ROC zooms appear in Figure 1. On split A, TPR@{0.5, 1, 2}% equals {1.000, 1.000, 1.000} with 95% bootstrap CIs [1.000, 1.000] at all points (AUC = 1.000, AUPRC = 1.000, ECE (Platt) = 0.051). On split B, TPR@{0.5, 1, 2}% is {0.572, 0.601, 0.633} with CIs [0.535, 0.591], [0.583, 0.618], [0.618, 0.657] (AUC = 0.953, AUPRC = 0.965, ECE (Platt) = 0.135). On TON_IoT, TPR@{0.5, 1, 2}% is {0.142, 0.196, 0.316} with CIs [0.124, 0.171], [0.183, 0.208], [0.293, 0.337] (AUC = 0.880, AUPRC = 0.602, ECE (Platt) = 0.103). Isotonic calibration improves ECE to 0.003/0.014/0.077 on A/B/TON without altering ROC/TPR (monotonicity). Because thresholds are transferred from A val, realized FPRs on B and TON_IoT may differ from the target 1%; we therefore do not report expected FP counts here. All CIs use n = 1000 non parametric bootstrap resamples [26]. Unless stated otherwise, metrics report the XGBoost detector; LR follows the same trends; omitted for space.
The near perfect separation on split A likely reflects a favorable class/operating mix rather than leakage. The z scaler, thresholds, and calibrators are fit on A train/A val only, and devices/times are disjoint across A/B.
4.2. Calibration After Isotonic Scaling
Transferring the isotonic calibrator trained on A val yields large ECE reductions vs. Platt scaling on all domains: split A 0.051 → 0.003 (–94%), split B 0.135 → 0.014 (–90%), TON_IoT 0.103 → 0.077 (–25%). Because isotonic is monotone, ROC/TPR at fixed FPR are unchanged; reliability diagrams are shown in Figure 2 [9], [10], [5].
4.3. Calibration Under Input Drift
We perturb standardized inputs by radius r ∈ [0, 1] along three directions: a reproducible random unit vector and two detector aligned linear stresses (adv_linear+, adv_linear–). After isotonic scaling, split A remains essentially invariant (ECE ≈ 0.003 for all r). On split B, random drift is benign (ECE 0.014 → 0.010), adv_linear– is modest (ECE 0.014 → 0.021), and adv_linear+ steadily degrades calibration (ECE 0.014 → 0.155). On TON_IoT, adv_linear+ raises ECE from 0.077 to 0.168, while adv_linear– improves it to 0.032; random increases mildly to 0.102 (Figures 3–4).
4.4. Tightness and Coverage of Value–Stability Certificates
Figure 5 plots the ratio ΔV* / B(r) (empirical drop vs. certified budget). Across split A, split B, and TON_IoT, tightness is 10^2–10^3 below 1.0, confirming non vacuity; local median bounds are 10–100× tighter than global bounds while maintaining coverage near 1.0. Curves are flat in r, indicating approximately linear scaling of empirical drops with drift radius.
4.5. Certificate Decomposition and Dominant Contributors
With γ = 0.90 (K_R = 10, K_P = 180), support size k = 4, and |S| = 8, the certificate decomposes as:
B_global(r) = (K_R L_R + K_P k L_P) r, B_local(r) = (K_R L_R^med + K_P k L_P^med) r.
At r = 0.5, the transition term dominates in both regimes (e.g., local median B_R ≈ 0.3 vs. B_P ≈ 118.4; global B_R ≈ 5.0 vs. B_P ≈ 359.3), reflecting the role of k and L_P (Figures 6–7).
4.6.  Effect of Drift Direction
On split B, decoder-aligned (adversarial) directions produce the largest ΔV*, random isotropic directions the smallest, and PCA 1 intermediate; gaps widen with r (Figure 8). On TON_IoT, raw ΔV*(r) remains far below the theoretical ceiling (10 at γ = 0.90), and local median tightness/coverage remain conservative yet non-vacuous across r (Figure 9).
4.7. Sensitivity Analyses
Varying γ: As γ ↑, K_R and K_P increase, steepening both global and local lines; coverage at fixed r typically decreases (Figure 10).
Varying support size k: The transition contribution scales linearly with k; we use k = 4 by default and report k ∈ {2, 4, 6} as a check (Figure 11).
Varying |S|: Holding Lipschitz factors fixed, (K_R, K_P) are independent of |S|; bound slopes are invariant (Figure 12).

4.8. Device Level Behavior
Per device analysis shows most devices operate far from the bound (median tightness at r = 0.5 in the 10−4–10−3 range), with split A exhibiting near zero ECE, split B moderate dispersion, and TON_IoT a few high ECE outliers. Worst case device–split pairs at r = 1.0 remain < 10−2 (Figures 13–15).

4.9. Ablations on Global Lipschitz Fctors
We measure global L_R and L_P and recompute coverage curves; Table 3 reports constants and AUCC (area under the coverage curve across radii). Local median variants (not shown in the table) further tighten bounds without sacrificing coverage.
4.10. Sensitivity to Window Overlap
We reduce effective overlap via per device, time ordered Bernoulli sub sampling (keep_prob = 1 − overlap). On split B, detection is stable up to 25% reduction (TPR@1% ≈ 0.845–0.849, AUPRC ≈ 0.97); at 50%, the population shrinks and low FPR degrades (TPR@0.5% ↓ to 0.304; TPR@1% ↓ to 0.608). Certificate coverage at r = 0.7 remains near 1.0 (Figure 16).

4.11. Projection/Decoder Ablations and Coverage
Projection choices mainly affect conservativeness, not validity. Figure 17 summarizes: (A) certified r budgets, (B) tightness vs. r, and (C–D) coverage vs. r (with small r zoom). Local median projections consistently tighten bounds while keeping coverage ≈ 1.0 in the operational regime.


4.12. Statistical Significance and Effect Sizes
We compare TPR@τ_t (A val thresholds) using bootstrap CIs. Because A, B, and TON_IoT are disjoint test sets, rank tests assuming pairing are not strictly appropriate; we therefore treat them as descriptive only, and rely on bootstrap CIs for inference. Split A significantly exceeds split B at all three operating points (median ΔTPR = {+0.224, +0.209, +0.178}; all p_Holm < 10^−6), and both A and B significantly exceed TON_IoT (all p_Holm < 10^−6). Effect sizes confirm large gaps: Vargha–Delaney A_12 = 1.00 (Cliff’s δ = 1.00) throughout [30], [31] (Tables 4–5).

4.13. Verification Artifacts
Decoder verification confirms the assumptions used by the certificates: all linear layers are 1 Lipschitz (operator norms ≤ 1), transition rows are stochastic with exact fixed support size k = 4 (via ε floor), and out of support mass is negligible. Aggregated checks are provided in Tables 6–8.

4.14. One Sentence Takeaway
At FPR = {0.5, 1, 2}%, we achieve TPR = {0.572, 0.601, 0.633} on split B and {0.142, 0.196, 0.316} on TON_IoT (split A is near perfect at all three points); isotonic calibration markedly reduces ECE while preserving ROC/TPR, and our certified value–stability bounds are conservative but non vacuous, with empirical drops 10–100× below budget and coverage near 1.0 across radii.
5. DISCUSSION
5.1. Operating at Low FPR in Practice

Security operations centers (SOCs) run detectors at very low false–positive rates (FPR) because attacks are rare and analyst attention is scarce [1]. Our protocol fixes thresholds on split A validation negatives and transfers them unchanged to all test sets, mirroring deployment rather than post–hoc tuning. At these frozen operating points, the detectors sustain strong true–positive rates (TPR) on split B—{0.572, 0.601, 0.633} at FPR = {0.5, 1, 2}%—but drop further on the cross–domain TON_IoT ({0.142, 0.196, 0.316}); split A is near–perfect and serves mainly as an internal check (Table 2, Fig. 1). Because thresholds are transferred from A‑val, realized FPRs on B and TON_IoT may deviate from the target 1%; we therefore do not report expected FP counts for those domains. Threshold transfer thus provides reproducibility and operational clarity, but it is intentionally harsher under shift, where fixed operating points expose real performance budgets rather than allowing retuning.

5.2. Generalization and Domain Shift

The A→B→TON_IoT gradient reflects increasing distributional mismatch: unseen devices and malware families by construction, different protocol/application mixes, and in TON_IoT, heterogeneous telemetry with some engineered features missing and imputed with zeros before the common z–scaler (Sec. III‑C). These factors can depress sensitivity in the extreme low–FPR region even when global ranking metrics remain high (e.g., B: ROC–AUC = 0.953, PR–AUC = 0.965). Our robustness probes help reconcile this: curves in Fig. 3 and Fig. 4 show that small but aligned drifts can shift score distributions just enough to cross fixed thresholds, reducing TPR@low–FPR while leaving ROC/PR largely intact [2], [3], [18]. In short, threshold transfer surfaces the sensitivity of the operating regime we actually care about.

5.3. Calibration as a First–Class Objective

Because isotonic regression is monotone, it preserves ROC curves and TPR at fixed FPR while correcting probability miscalibration [9], [10], [5]. Consistent with this, ECE drops from 0.051 → 0.003 (A), 0.135 → 0.014 (B), and 0.103 → 0.077 (TON_IoT) without altering the low–FPR operating characteristics (Table 2; reliability diagrams in Fig. 2). Under controlled drift, calibration behavior depends on direction: on B, random drift is benign (ECE 0.014 → 0.010), the adv_linear– direction is modest (to 0.021), while detector–aligned adv_linear+ steadily degrades calibration to ≈ 0.155 at r = 1 (Fig. 3, 4). On TON_IoT, adv_linear+ raises ECE from 0.077 to ≈ 0.168; adv_linear– improves it to ≈ 0.032; random increases mildly to ≈ 0.102. These patterns argue both for keeping calibration as a first–class metric and for monitoring it over time in deployment.


5.4. Certification

The Lipschitz–constrained, row–local decoder yields a value–stability certificate: for standardized perturbations of radius r, the change in optimal MDP value is bounded by B(r) (Eq. (1)). Operationally, this means small feature drifts cannot induce large swings in the decoded “behavior score,” providing analysts with a more stable summary than raw classifier scores. This guarantee is not a label–stability certificate and does not prevent class flips per se; it bounds how much the decoded dynamics can change. Empirically, bounds are conservative but non–vacuous: tightness ratios ΔV* / B(r) are 10^2–10^3× below 1 with coverage near 1.0 across radii and splits (Fig. 5). Local–median Lipschitz estimates tighten bounds by 10–100× over global ones while retaining coverage. Decomposition shows why: with γ = 0.90 (K_R = 10, K_P = 180) and support k = 4, the transition term dominates (e.g., at r = 0.5, local–median B_R ≈ 0.3 vs. B_P ≈ 118.4; global B_R ≈ 5.0 vs. B_P ≈ 359.3), and on TON_IoT the transition channel accounts for ≈ 99.8% of B(r) at r = 1.0 (Figs. 6, 7). Design implication: architectural or regularization effort spent on stabilizing the transition head (and managing its effective support k) buys the largest certificate gains [11], [12], [13], [14], [15].

5.5. Sensitivity and Ablations (γ, k, |S|, Overlap)

Sensitivity studies align with the theory. Increasing γ steepens the bound via K_R = 1 / (1 − γ) and K_P = 2γ / (1 − γ)^2; increasing support size k scales the transition contribution linearly (Figs. 10, 11). Holding Lipschitz factors fixed, changing the state count |S| leaves the slope invariant (Fig. 12). Reducing window overlap by per–device Bernoulli sub–sampling is tolerable up to ≈ 25% reduction (on B, TPR@1% stays ≈ 0.845–0.849), after which population shrinkage hurts low–FPR performance (at 50% reduction: TPR@0.5% = 0.304, TPR@1% = 0.608) while certificate coverage remains near unity (Fig. 16).

5.6. Threats to Validity and Limitations

Both IoT–23 and TON_IoT may contain labeling noise, coverage gaps, or temporal artifacts that affect generalization. Near–perfect separation on A likely reflects a favorable mix of devices and attacks, not leakage; scalers/calibrators were fit on A–train/A–val only, and devices/families are disjoint across splits (Sec. III‑B).
We use flow–level tabular features; no payload analysis or binary scanning is performed. The guarantee is on the value stability of the decoded MDP, not on classification stability or adversarial robustness; tightness is dominated by the transition channel under typical settings. Even with isotonic, calibration degrades along detector–aligned directions on B and TON_IoT; monitoring and periodic recalibration may be required [18].


6. CONCLUSION
We set out from a practical constraint: intrusion detectors for IoT have to run at very low false–positive rates and still return calibrated probabilities that analysts can trust. To reflect that reality, we adopt a simple, deployment faithful protocol—choose operating thresholds once on split A validation negatives and transfer them unchanged to all test conditions—and pair strong but transparent detectors (logistic regression and XGBoost with post hoc calibration) with a Lipschitz constrained, row local decoder. The decoder maps each 300 s window to a compact MDP and provides a deterministic value–stability guarantee (see Eq. (1)), so small standardized drifts cannot cause large swings in the decoded behavior score. We evaluate on device  and family disjoint splits of IoT 23 and under domain shift on TON_IoT, using leak free preprocessing and explicit verification artifacts.
Under fixed thresholds, the evidence is clear. On split B, TPR at FPR = {0.5, 1, 2}% is {0.572, 0.601, 0.633}; on TON_IoT it is {0.142, 0.196, 0.316}. Isotonic calibration, being monotone, preserves ROC/TPR while improving ECE to 0.003/0.014/0.077 on A/B/TON_IoT, respectively. Certificates are conservative but non vacuous: empirical value changes sit roughly 10–100× below the allotted budget with coverage ≈ 1.0 across radii, and decomposition shows the transition channel dominates the bound at γ = 0.90 with support k = 4.
Our guarantee targets value stability of the decoded dynamics, not label stability, and cross domain generalization at low FPR remains challenging. That said, the approach is auditable and extensible: we release per layer operator norms, row stochasticity checks (with fixed support), measured Lipschitz constants (global and local median), and the frozen thresholds used for evaluation. These artifacts make it straightforward to reproduce results, to monitor calibration in the field, and to tighten bounds further—especially by focusing effort on stabilizing the transition head—while future work explores low FPR domain adaptation and online/conformal calibration under drift. As future work, two directions stand out. First, domain adaptation for low–FPR operation, including threshold selection and calibration transfer, could reduce the A→B→TON_IoT gap without post–hoc tuning. Second, online or conformal calibration under drift may keep probabilities reliable between retrains. On the certified layer, richer yet verifiable decoders (alternative Lipschitz controls, dynamic or learned k, structured sparsity) and joint training where detector and decoder co-regularize are promising. Finally, integrating protocol/context features while preserving the certificate is an open design problem.

